--- a/specs/state.md
+++ b/specs/state.md
@@ -245,6 +245,42 @@ Checksum and program-identity compatibility:
 - Equality of step, checkpoint, call-frame, or other persisted identities is not by itself evidence that a run can resume across changed source. The root workflow checksum remains an independent compatibility guard.
 - Any future cross-source compatibility mechanism must be a tested atomic upgrader that owns both checksum and program-identity compatibility, validates the complete old-to-new transition, and either commits the compatible state as one operation or leaves the old state unchanged. Evidence records, identity deltas, aliases, or partial remaps are not such an upgrader.
 
+## Checksum-compatible resume projection integrity
+
+A run whose workflow source still has the same root checksum may carry persisted identities the current program no longer owns: a stale or renamed step id, a loop iteration that cannot exist, a call frame whose caller or import alias changed, or two resumable frames for one call. Resume therefore audits every scope it will execute against the current workflow projection and fails closed before any mutation or effect. State schema stays `"2.1"`; pre-v2.0 state and pre-2.1 reusable-call state remain rejected. The audit reads no migration, retirement or parity evidence, keeps no receipts and no persisted audit cache.
+
+Audit points and ordering:
+- `audit_scope(bundle, state, scope_path)` in `orchestrator.workflow.resume_projection_integrity` is the only definition of "owned". It reads nothing but the bundle and state it is given (no workflow or bundle loading, no checksum, no file read) and never mutates the audited state.
+- `orchestrator resume` audits the persisted root scope after schema and root-checksum acceptance and before observability overrides, executor session or process metadata, executor construction and the execution prologue. On failure it exits 1, prints the reason to standard error and persists only the failure envelope below. The initial root-checksum precheck is unchanged (exit 1, nothing written).
+- A `WorkflowExecutor` that resumes as the structural root (not a call-frame executor) recomputes the checksum through `StateManager.calculate_checksum`, then audits the root scope, immediately before its prologue. A call-frame executor does not repeat the root guard.
+- A reached call revalidates its frame shape, caller boundary, persisted import alias and lineage, after the parent call visit/start publication and after authored-input, managed/runtime-input, write-root, checksum and applicable resume-bound-input validation, but before the callee frame or state is created or changed and before any callee effect. The persisted caller id is resolved against the current parent projection with one `resolve_call_boundary(slot_index, call_step_id)` per reached call identity; exactly one current boundary must own it and that boundary's current import alias selects the callee bundle (the persisted alias is checked, it never redirects selection). Nested scopes apply the same rule recursively.
+
+Identities:
+- Every present explicit durable step identity (`steps.<name>.step_id`, `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id`) must resolve to exactly one candidate in its owning scope. Resume never splits, normalizes, prefix-matches, reconstructs, remaps or backfills ids and never selects by workflow, module or procedure name, basename, path suffix or persisted alias. Loop-local and loop-contained call ids resolve only through projection-owned candidates for iterations the loop bookkeeping makes legal.
+- An absent `steps.<name>.step_id` stays compatible for every schema-valid row the presentation/name/order fallback supports, whatever its status; resume leaves the field absent. `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.*.call_step_id`, call-frame current position and loop progress selectors are mandatory.
+- `for_each` bookkeeping: indices are unique non-boolean non-negative integers within `items`; `current_index` is in range and not completed. `repeat_until` admits four forms: active; terminal condition success; terminal successful exhaustion (completed `on_exhausted` outputs, `exhausted: true`, completed frame); terminal failed exhaustion (`exhausted` absent or false, failed frame with `error.type` `repeat_until_iterations_exhausted`). Terminal forms have `current_iteration: null`, keep the completed-iteration history and identify the terminal evaluated iteration; exhaustion covers the declared maximum range.
+
+Call frames and Workflow Lisp retry lineage:
+- Every frame validates caller identity, import alias, boundary ownership and status. Completed frames are unlimited historical records and never resumable. A boundary whose callee is not Workflow Lisp has at most one non-completed frame.
+- A Workflow Lisp boundary is recognised only by the typed capability `provenance.frontend_kind == "workflow_lisp"` (never by suffix or name). Its non-completed frames form one lineage: base frame `B` (ordinal 0) and retries `B::retry::<n>`, with zero or one running member and any number of failed predecessors. Mixed bases, several running members, a duplicate or missing ordinal, a malformed or nested marker, an unknown status, or a caller or alias differing from the boundary's fail closed. The next fresh retry id is the lowest unused positive ordinal over all members, completed included.
+- With a running member: its checksum and resume-bound-input validation run first and win on failure; then every failed predecessor is checksum-validated and audited in ordinal order; then the running member's own scope is audited and resumed. Without one, every failed predecessor is checksum-validated and audited before the fresh retry frame is allocated.
+- A completed frame whose id equals the frame a resumed call would occupy is rejected before any lineage grouping with `call_resume_state_invalid` / `completed_call_frame_id_collision`; no child manager or executor is created and frames are unchanged.
+
+Diagnostic (`resume_projection_integrity_error`, schema `resume_projection_integrity_error.v1`):
+- `error = {"type": "resume_projection_integrity_error", "message": "Resume projection integrity audit failed: <reason>", "context": {...}}`; `context` always carries `diagnostic_schema`, `reason`, `scope_path` (identity-only: `{"kind": "root", "workflow_file"}` then `{"kind": "call_frame", "frame_id"}` per frame entered), `field`, `offending_value` (scalar or null; containers never shown), `expected_owner` (`workflow_file`, `workflow_checksum`, `projection_scope` of `root` or `call_frame`), `candidate_count` and `call_boundary_step_id`.
+- Reasons: `unknown_explicit_step_id`, `presentation_slot_mismatch`, `out_of_scope_step_id`, `unclaimed_explicit_step_row`, `missing_required_identity`, `missing_call_boundary`, `ambiguous_call_boundary`, `ambiguous_resumable_call_frame`, `persisted_import_alias_mismatch`, `missing_imported_bundle`, `unsupported_shape`, `invalid_loop_progress`.
+- Diagnostics never expose bound inputs, context values, artifacts, prompts, provider output, secrets, whole frame state or projections, or the contents of a malformed list or mapping used as an identity.
+
+Failure envelopes:
+- A structural root whose checksum recheck fails persists `error = {"type": "workflow_checksum_mismatch", "message": "Workflow has been modified since the run started", "context": {"workflow_file", "persisted_checksum", "current_checksum", "reason"}}` with `reason` one of `workflow_modified`, `missing_recorded_checksum`, `missing_workflow_path`, `workflow_unavailable`. It changes only root `status` (`failed`), `error` and `updated_at`, and stops before the projection audit and the prologue. A root projection failure (CLI or executor) changes only the same three fields. Already-open session/process metadata may remain and a failing session close cannot replace the diagnostic.
+- An unchanged `current_step` left by either envelope is forensic: the run is reported failed with its error, the status snapshot shows that step as `pending` (not `running`, not `in progress`), and heartbeat and stalled-run interpretation do not treat it as live.
+- A reached-callee failure before the callee is constructed becomes the caller's failed call-step result and the scope/run error; the callee frame and state stay untouched; ancestors persist ordinary failed step/current/visit/frame snapshots and promote the same diagnostic, unchanged, to the root. It is never wrapped in `call_failed` or a loop wrapper.
+- `resume_projection_integrity_error` is sticky and non-routable: authored `success`, `failure` and `always` routes, `on_error=continue` and `for_each`/`repeat_until` continuation cannot consume it. Configured finalization may still run, but a finalization failure is supplemental: it is recorded under `finalization` with `status: failed` and `failure = {"step", "step_id", "error"}` (`error` is the cleanup's own projection error, or null for an ordinary failure) and never wraps, replaces, clears or downgrades the run's projection diagnostic. A projection error arising only inside finalization becomes the run error and `finalization.failure.error`.
+
+Classic-YAML import aliases are checked before bundle construction: an alias occurring more than once among effective `imports` entries (duplicate keys, a `<<` merge inside `imports`, repeated `imports` sections, or several merge sources carrying `imports`) is reported once as `imports.<alias>: duplicate import alias` with the `line L, column C` of each occurrence; repeated `imports` sections with distinct aliases are reported once as `imports: duplicate top-level mapping` with the position of each `imports` key. A single merged `imports` section with unique aliases loads.
+
+Every bundle produced by the Workflow Lisp compiler (imported Workflow Lisp bundles and lowered bundles included) carries `provenance.frontend_kind == "workflow_lisp"`; classic-YAML bundles passed in `imported_workflow_bundles` keep their own provenance and are linked as the same object.
+
 Workflow Lisp lexical-checkpoint default resume:
 - Node-local restore selection is primary. A prior-boundary fallback is allowed only when the restart node owns lexical checkpoint metadata and restore selection positively reports typed `record_absent` for that node's next boundary.
 - Only the canonical checkpoint index whose `program_point_id` matches the runtime-plan point and whose `storage_allocation_id` matches the canonical lexical-checkpoint-index allocation may establish absence. A missing canonical index or a valid canonical index with an empty `records` list is `record_absent`; a present unreadable, malformed, incomplete, foreign, stale, or otherwise invalid index is `record_present_unusable` and fails closed with a stable diagnostic.
